{"id":"newtons-laws","title":"Physics: motion and Newton's laws","subject":"Physics","label":"JAMB · WAEC","exams":["jamb","waec"],"language":"en","seconds":175,"duration":175,"audio":"voice.mp3","words":453,"sections":[{"id":"hook","title":"Everyday motion","start":0,"end":12.285},{"id":"explain","title":"Speed and velocity","start":12.285,"end":34.785},{"id":"explain2","title":"Newton's laws","start":34.785,"end":77.205},{"id":"example","title":"Worked example","start":77.205,"end":101.135},{"id":"check","title":"Quick check","start":101.135,"end":110.175},{"id":"answer","title":"Answer","start":110.175,"end":118.565},{"id":"mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","start":118.565,"end":146.035},{"id":"recap","title":"Recap","start":146.035,"end":175}],"lines":[{"s":0,"a":0.5,"b":3.9,"text":"Why do you jerk forward when a danfo bus brakes suddenly?","w":[0.54,0.76,0.86,1.04,1.38,1.88,2.06,2.16,2.66,2.92,3.4]},{"s":0,"a":4.35,"b":11.86,"text":"I'm Kora. You already feel these laws every day, and today we'll make motion and Newton's laws simple for JAMB and WAEC.","w":[4.48,4.68,5.66,5.84,6.22,6.5,6.74,7.18,7.42,8.1,8.22,8.58,8.78,8.98,9.44,9.6,9.96,10.48,10.84,11.04,11.34,11.46]},{"s":1,"a":12.71,"b":19.72,"text":"Speed is distance travelled per unit time. Velocity is speed in a stated direction, so direction matters.","w":[12.82,13.18,13.32,13.74,14.18,14.44,14.68,15.5,16.16,16.3,16.76,16.86,16.94,17.3,18.38,18.72,19.2]},{"s":1,"a":20.02,"b":26.64,"text":"Acceleration is change in velocity divided by time. We write it as a equals v minus u all over t.","w":[20.1,20.9,21.06,21.38,21.48,22.12,22.56,22.76,23.68,23.88,24.12,24.3,24.44,24.64,25.06,25.26,25.7,25.96,26.14,26.36]},{"s":1,"a":26.94,"b":34.36,"text":"The unit is metres per second squared. If Ada walks to market, her speed tells how fast. Her velocity also says which way.","w":[26.96,27.18,27.44,27.6,27.96,28.14,28.44,29.26,29.54,29.82,30.14,30.26,30.94,31.14,31.48,31.74,31.94,32.74,32.88,33.46,33.72,33.94,34.12]},{"s":2,"a":35.21,"b":43.88,"text":"Newton's first law says a body stays at rest, or moves with uniform velocity in a straight line, unless a resultant external force acts on it.","w":[35.32,35.72,36.04,36.3,36.56,36.64,36.96,37.32,37.44,38.22,38.4,38.74,38.98,39.42,40.02,40.12,40.2,40.5,41.34,41.7,41.78,42.42,42.92,43.36,43.62,43.76]},{"s":2,"a":44.18,"b":49.83,"text":"That is inertia. In a danfo, when the driver brakes, your body keeps moving, so you jerk forward.","w":[44.2,44.44,44.6,45.6,45.72,45.82,46.68,46.84,46.96,47.36,47.68,47.86,48.18,48.48,48.9,49.08,49.22,49.5]},{"s":2,"a":50.13,"b":54.88,"text":"The second law says the rate of change of momentum is proportional to the resultant force.","w":[50.16,50.32,50.66,50.94,51.2,51.36,51.62,51.76,52.08,52.18,52.82,52.96,53.64,53.78,53.88,54.4]},{"s":2,"a":55.18,"b":62.07,"text":"For constant mass, force equals mass times acceleration, in newtons. Momentum is mass times velocity.","w":[55.2,55.38,55.84,56.64,57.04,57.38,57.72,58.04,59.12,59.28,60.08,60.7,60.9,61.2,61.52]},{"s":2,"a":62.37,"b":68.12,"text":"The third law says to every action there is an equal and opposite reaction, acting on different bodies.","w":[62.4,62.56,62.84,63.1,63.42,63.6,63.94,64.34,64.54,64.64,64.84,65.24,65.44,65.8,66.9,67.22,67.34,67.62]},{"s":2,"a":68.42,"b":71.86,"text":"A rocket pushes gas down, and the gas pushes the rocket up.","w":[68.48,68.58,69,69.36,69.68,70.3,70.4,70.52,70.9,71.24,71.36,71.74]},{"s":2,"a":72.16,"b":76.78,"text":"When you walk, your foot pushes the ground back, and the ground pushes you forward.","w":[72.2,72.38,72.56,73.32,73.54,73.78,74.14,74.24,74.58,75.24,75.34,75.46,75.82,76.16,76.32]},{"s":3,"a":77.63,"b":85.14,"text":"Let's use a car of mass one thousand kilograms. It accelerates from rest to twenty metres per second in ten seconds.","w":[77.7,78.02,78.18,78.24,78.5,78.64,79.18,79.42,79.96,81.36,81.48,82.16,82.4,82.68,82.82,83.14,83.46,83.64,84.14,84.28,84.58]},{"s":3,"a":85.44,"b":93.46,"text":"Acceleration equals change in velocity divided by time. That is twenty minus zero, all over ten, which gives two metres per second squared.","w":[85.52,86.46,86.84,87.18,87.28,87.9,88.32,88.48,89.44,89.62,89.78,90.12,90.46,90.9,91.08,91.28,91.56,91.78,92.06,92.26,92.58,92.76,93.06]},{"s":3,"a":93.76,"b":100.71,"text":"Force equals mass times acceleration. So force equals one thousand times two, which is two thousand newtons.","w":[93.82,94.3,94.66,95.04,95.44,96.66,96.88,97.28,97.66,97.82,98.26,98.66,99.28,99.5,99.64,99.86,100.22]},{"s":4,"a":101.56,"b":107.28,"text":"Quick check. What force gives a mass of five kilograms an acceleration of four metres per second squared?","w":[101.62,101.86,102.64,102.88,103.2,103.44,103.52,103.82,103.96,104.28,104.82,104.92,105.7,105.88,106.16,106.48,106.62,106.92]},{"s":4,"a":107.73,"b":109.75,"text":"Pause here and try it. I'll wait.","w":[107.74,108.08,108.3,108.42,108.72,109.24,109.44]},{"s":5,"a":110.6,"b":118.14,"text":"The answer is twenty newtons. Force equals mass times acceleration, so five kilograms times four gives twenty newtons.","w":[110.62,110.9,111.32,111.5,111.86,112.9,113.32,113.68,114.02,114.32,115.42,115.64,115.96,116.56,116.88,117.22,117.46,117.78]},{"s":6,"a":118.99,"b":123.11,"text":"Three common mistakes. First, saying action and reaction cancel.","w":[119.02,119.36,119.7,120.7,121.28,121.68,122.06,122.22,122.72]},{"s":6,"a":123.41,"b":127.04,"text":"They act on different bodies, so they can never cancel on one body.","w":[123.46,123.78,124,124.14,124.48,125.14,125.3,125.44,125.62,125.92,126.34,126.54,126.68]},{"s":6,"a":127.34,"b":137.43,"text":"Second, confusing mass with weight. Mass is in kilograms; weight is a force in newtons, and weight equals mass times g, the acceleration due to gravity.","w":[127.44,128.18,128.8,129.06,129.22,130.14,130.48,130.6,130.72,131.82,132.1,132.24,132.36,132.72,132.82,133.66,133.84,134.2,134.6,134.92,135.26,135.96,136.1,136.76,136.9,137]},{"s":6,"a":137.73,"b":145.61,"text":"Third, forgetting units or using speed where direction matters. Always write metres per second, metres per second squared, and newtons.","w":[137.78,138.46,138.9,139.28,139.46,139.76,140.1,140.3,140.72,141.68,142.04,142.32,142.68,142.86,143.58,143.9,144.04,144.34,144.98,145.12]},{"s":7,"a":146.46,"b":153.7,"text":"Recap. Speed is distance per time, velocity adds direction, and acceleration is change in velocity over time.","w":[146.5,147.46,147.8,147.96,148.34,148.52,149.3,149.92,150.12,151.02,151.16,151.92,152.1,152.44,152.54,153.14,153.36]},{"s":7,"a":154,"b":162.55,"text":"Newton's first law is inertia, the second gives force equals mass times acceleration, and the third gives equal and opposite reactions on different bodies.","w":[154.12,154.52,154.82,155.16,155.28,156.16,156.32,156.68,156.94,157.28,157.62,157.9,158.2,159.28,159.4,159.58,159.96,160.3,160.62,160.8,161.16,161.72,161.84,162.1]},{"s":7,"a":162.85,"b":170.61,"text":"Exam tip: show the formula, substitute, then write the unit. And remember, momentum is mass times velocity.","w":[162.96,163.34,164.12,164.34,164.46,165.3,166.24,166.5,166.72,166.88,167.72,167.84,168.66,169.2,169.38,169.74,170.04]},{"s":7,"a":170.91,"b":174.4,"text":"Keep practising with Knowzone, and these questions will feel simple.","w":[170.92,171.18,171.74,171.92,172.78,172.9,173.16,173.64,173.82,174.04]}],"scenes":[{"at":0.25,"heading":"Everyday motion","items":[{"at":0.4,"kind":"text","text":"Danfo brakes suddenly → you jerk forward?"},{"at":4.25,"kind":"text","text":"JAMB/WAEC: motion + Newton's laws"}]},{"at":12.46,"heading":"Speed and velocity","items":[{"at":12.61,"kind":"term","text":"Speed","detail":"Distance travelled per unit time"},{"at":12.61,"kind":"term","text":"Velocity","detail":"Speed in a stated direction; direction matters"},{"at":19.92,"kind":"formula","text":"a = (v − u) ÷ t"},{"at":26.84,"kind":"text","text":"Unit: m/s²; velocity states direction"}]},{"at":34.96,"heading":"Newton's laws I & II","items":[{"at":35.11,"kind":"term","text":"First law","detail":"Body stays at rest/uniform velocity unless resultant force acts"},{"at":44.08,"kind":"term","text":"Inertia","detail":"Body keeps moving when bus brakes; you jerk forward"},{"at":50.03,"kind":"term","text":"Second law","detail":"Rate of change of momentum proportional to resultant force"},{"at":55.08,"kind":"formula","text":"F = ma; p = mv"}]},{"at":62.12,"heading":"Newton's third law","items":[{"at":62.27,"kind":"term","text":"Third law","detail":"Equal and opposite reaction on different bodies"},{"at":68.32,"kind":"text","text":"Rocket: gas down → rocket up"},{"at":72.06,"kind":"text","text":"Walk: foot pushes ground back → you move forward"}]},{"at":77.38,"heading":"Worked example: car","items":[{"at":77.53,"kind":"text","text":"m = 1000 kg; u = 0; v = 20 m/s; t = 10 s"},{"at":85.34,"kind":"step","text":"a = (20 − 0) ÷ 10 = 2 m/s²"},{"at":93.66,"kind":"step","text":"F = ma = 1000 × 2 = 2000 N"}]},{"at":101.14,"heading":"Quick check","items":[{"at":101.14,"kind":"question","text":"What force gives a mass of 5 kg an acceleration of 4 m/s²?"}]},{"at":110.35,"heading":"Answer","items":[{"at":110.5,"kind":"text","text":"Answer: 20 N"},{"at":110.5,"kind":"formula","text":"F = ma = 5 × 4 = 20 N"}]},{"at":118.74,"heading":"Common mistakes","items":[{"at":118.89,"kind":"text","text":"Three common mistakes"},{"at":123.31,"kind":"wrong","text":"Action and reaction cancel"},{"at":127.24,"kind":"wrong","text":"Mass = weight"},{"at":137.63,"kind":"text","text":"Write m/s, m/s², N; direction matters"}]},{"at":146.21,"heading":"Recap","items":[{"at":146.36,"kind":"text","text":"Speed = distance/time; velocity adds direction; a = (v−u)/t"},{"at":153.9,"kind":"text","text":"1st: inertia; 2nd: F = ma; 3rd: equal/opposite"},{"at":162.75,"kind":"formula","text":"p = mv"},{"at":162.75,"kind":"tip","text":"Show formula, substitute, then write unit"},{"at":170.81,"kind":"text","text":"Practise with Knowzone"}]}],"check":{"at":110,"seconds":10,"question":"What force gives a mass of 5 kg an acceleration of 4 m/s²?","answer":"20 N (F = ma = 5 × 4)"},"fps":30,"mouth":"","level":"","credits":[],"voice":"qwen3-tts-12hz-1.7b (cloned voice)"}